#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:1
msgid "Never type fallback change"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:3
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:5
msgid ""
"\\[!NOTE\\] The changes previously documented in this chapter are no longer "
"edition-specific as of Rust 1.100."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:8
msgid ""
"Never type (`!`) to any type (\"never-to-any\") coercions now fall back to "
"never type (`!`) rather than to unit type (`()`) on all editions."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:10
msgid ""
"The `dependency_on_unit_never_type_fallback` lint, which handled the "
"migration, has also been removed."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:12
msgid ""
"The description of \"never-to-any\" coercions below is kept for "
"informational purposes."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:14
msgid "Details"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:16
msgid ""
"When the compiler sees a value of type `!` (never) in a [coercion site]"
"(../../reference/type-coercions.html#coercion-sites), it implicitly inserts "
"a coercion to allow the type checker to infer any type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:19
#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:33
msgid "// This:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:21
msgid "// ...is (essentially) turned by the compiler into:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:24
msgid ""
"// ...where `absurd` is the following function\n"
"// (it's sound because `!` always marks unreachable code):\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:30
msgid "This can lead to compilation errors if the type cannot be inferred:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:36
msgid "// ...gets turned into this:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:38
msgid "//~ ERROR can't infer the type of `absurd`\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:41
msgid ""
"To prevent such errors, the compiler remembers where it inserted `absurd` "
"calls, and if it can't infer the type, it uses the fallback type instead:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:45
msgid "/* An arbitrarily selected type! */"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:49
msgid "This is what is known as \"never type fallback\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:51
msgid ""
"Historically, the fallback type has been `()` (unit).  This caused `!` to "
"spontaneously coerce to `()` even when the compiler would not infer `()` "
"without the fallback.  That was confusing and has prevented the "
"stabilization of the `!` type."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:53
msgid ""
"On all editions, the fallback type is now `!`.  This makes things work more "
"intuitively.  Now when you pass `!` and there is no reason to coerce it to "
"something else, it is kept as `!`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:55
msgid ""
"In some cases your code might depend on the fallback type being `()`, so "
"this can cause compilation errors or changes in behavior."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:59
msgid "Migration"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:61
msgid ""
"\\[!NOTE\\] Migration is no longer applicable because the never type now "
"falls back to `!` on all editions. The information on translating code that "
"assumed a fallback to `()` is preserved below in case you have legacy code "
"that needs updating."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:64
msgid ""
"There is no automatic fix to update code that assumed a fallback to `()`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:66
msgid ""
"The manual fix is to specify the type explicitly so that the fallback type "
"is not used.  Unfortunately, it might not be trivial to see which type needs "
"to be specified."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:68
msgid ""
"One of the most common patterns broken by this change is using `f()?;` where "
"`f` is generic over the `Ok`\\-part of the return type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:81
msgid ""
"You might think that, in this example, type `T` can't be inferred.  However, "
"due to the current desugaring of the `?` operator, it is now inferred as `!` "
"(whereas in previous versions of Rust it was inferred as `()`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:83
msgid "To fix the issue you need to specify the `T` type explicitly:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:90
#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:142
msgid "// ...or:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:97
msgid "Another relatively common case is panicking in a closure:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:110
msgid ""
"Previously `!` from the `panic!` coerced to `()` which implements `Unit`.  "
"However now the `!` is kept as `!` so this code fails because `!` doesn't "
"implement `Unit`.  To fix this you can specify the return type of the "
"closure:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:122
msgid ""
"A similar case to that of `f()?` can be seen when using a `!`\\-typed "
"expression in one branch and a function with an unconstrained return type in "
"the other:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:132
msgid ""
"Previously `()` was inferred as the return type of `Default::default()` "
"because `!` from `return` was spuriously coerced to `()`.  Now, `!` will be "
"inferred instead causing this code to not compile because `!` does not "
"implement `Default`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rust-2024/never-type-fallback.md:134
msgid "Again, this can be fixed by specifying the type explicitly:"
msgstr ""
